<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chao Cuo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 200–154 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Chao Cuo was a Chinese philosopher, politician and writer who served as an adviser and official under Emperor Wen and Emperor Jing of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a>. In a memorandum of 169 BCE he compared Han and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a> tactics and proposed settling government-supported migrants in walled towns on the northern frontier, to farm and train as militia; Emperor Wen approved it. He helped revive the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-documents">Classic of History</a>, taught by the daughter of the aged scholar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fu-sheng">Fu Sheng</a>, though later Eastern Han scholars called him a Legalist. Made Imperial Secretary in 155 BCE, he urged cutting down the princely kingdoms, and when seven kings rebelled his rivals persuaded Emperor Jing to have him executed in 154 BCE.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the Western Han most were small owner-cultivators, ranked above tenants and hired labourers: by the official <b>Chao Cuo</b>’s reckoning a family of five farmed no more than about 100 mu, some 11 acres.</blockquote><a href="/societies/han-china">Social Hierarchy of Han China</a></li></ul></section>
